### Root cause

The `cache` variable on line 12 of `main.go` is a plain `map[string]cacheEntry` — Go maps are not safe for concurrent use. Since `net/http` handles each request in its own goroutine, any concurrent traffic causes a data race that the Go runtime detects and panics on.

Note that `getHandler` also has a write path (`delete(cache, key)` for expired entries), so both handlers need write-lock protection.

### Recommended fix

Wrap the map with a `sync.RWMutex`:
- `RLock`/`RUnlock` for the read path in `getHandler` (when not evicting)
- `Lock`/`Unlock` for writes in `setHandler` and for the `delete` branch in `getHandler`

### Proposed test

```go
func TestConcurrentAccess(t *testing.T) {
    // Start test server
    // Spawn 100 goroutines hitting /set and /get concurrently
    // Run under go test -race
    // Assert: no panics, all responses return valid HTTP status codes
}
```
